# Last mile — client-ready exhibits

**Triggers.** Client-ready · last mile · exhibit · takeaway title · chart makeover ·
headline the chart · mix shift · put this in front of a client · slide · deck viz ·
so what.

Berinato (2019), “Data science and the art of persuasion.” Numbers still come from
`.cursor/rules/solving.mdc`. How those numbers are drawn is
`.cursor/rules/storytelling-with-data.mdc`. This rule is whether the exhibit
**persuades**. Analysis that a decision-maker does not understand, or does not find
persuasive, produces no decision — analytically it may as well not exist.

**One-line thesis.** A chart in consulting is not a picture of data. It is an argument.

## Non-negotiables

- **The message picks the chart, not the data.** Write the one sentence you are
  proving, then pick the comparison the reader needs in order to believe it. Do not
  ask what chart can show all the columns.
- **One message per exhibit.** If it needs a paragraph, it is two charts or none.
- **Takeaway title, not a topic title.** Formula: finding + direction + magnitude, in
  one sentence a CEO could repeat. Weak: “Mobile lines, 2024–2026.” Strong: “Mobile
  has added 1.8M lines in a year, but at a fraction of broadband’s margin.” A deck of
  titles, read in sequence, should be the storyline.
- **Visual impression matches the true magnitude.** Every design choice is a claim.
  Consultants make those claims deliberately; amateurs make them by accident.
- **One accent color, everything else gray.** Color spent everywhere is color spent
  nowhere. Highest-leverage makeover move.
- **Declutter means delete**, not prettify. Borders, gridlines, redundant legends,
  markers on every point, unused decimals, 3D — gone. Label the series on the chart.
- **Client exhibit persuades; appendix proves.** Both exist. They are different
  artifacts. Push detail back.
- **You own the exhibit** even if AI or Excel drew it. Machine defaults are clutter
  with a legend, mislabeled axes, and sometimes the wrong series. Same verification
  as Week 4; same ownership.

## Mix shift is a first-class message

Charter’s shape in this course: **total roughly flat, one line eroding, another
growing underneath.** Charting the flat total is having no story. The story is inside
the mix. Use indexed lines or a slopegraph. Dual axes let the designer manufacture
any story — do not.

## Axis rule (defensible, not mechanical)

| Chart | Baseline | Test |
|---|---|---|
| Bars | Zero. Length encodes value. | Truncating is lying. |
| Lines | May zoom if the story is movement in a tight band | Impression must match magnitude; caption says so. |

A truncated line axis is not automatically dishonest. A truncated bar axis is.

## Failure modes

- Redesigning before choosing the message (the makeover has no target).
- Keeping everything and just prettifying.
- Neutral titles “so we don’t look biased” — if you cannot state the takeaway, the
  chart is not ready. The title is the point; the chart is the evidence.
- “The data speaks for itself.” Unframed data transfers the interpretation to the
  client, who will skip it or do it differently than you intend.

## Pre-delivery gate — do not ship an exhibit until all are true

1. One sentence exists, and it is the title (finding + direction + magnitude).
2. That sentence is the only claim the visual has to prove.
3. Chart type matches the sentence (trend → line; rank → sorted bars; mix shift →
   indexed lines / slopegraph; one number → no chart).
4. One accent; the rest is gray.
5. Clutter is deleted, not restyled.
6. Source, units, and time range are on the exhibit; every number survives “where
   did that come from?”
7. A reader who sees only the title still gets the point.
8. Backup that proves the arithmetic lives in the appendix, not on this slide.
